// gfxdemo -- the first windowed program in `id`. It opens a 320x200 window via
// the native graphics backend (sys/win/gfx), then runs a frame loop that draws
// a moving box into a software framebuffer and presents it. The drawing is all
// portable `id`; only gfx_open/present/poll/close cross into platform code.
//
//   idc/bin/idc demos/gfxdemo --allow-untested -o gfxdemo && ./gfxdemo
//
// Close the window (or press Esc / q) to quit. Like the terminal engine, every
// function obeys id's rules -- 3 actions, 2-deep nesting, 3 functions per file.

// main() is not cased: gfx_open() succeeds whenever a real X display is
// reachable (true here -- a standalone XOpenDisplay probe confirmed it), and
// a successful open makes loop(ok) enter spin's live polling loop, which only
// ends via GFX_MAX_FRAMES, an environment variable no case can set and this
// build does not export. Casing it would hang for the 10s case cap on any
// machine with a reachable display and pass by accident on any machine
// without one -- not the same behaviour "on every machine, every run" that
// docs/TESTS.md's constraints section asks for, so it is left uncased rather
// than cased dishonestly.
main(int argc, string[] argv) {
  int ok = gfx_open(320, 200, "hey look ma i made it");
  sf_l_init(320, 200);
  loop(ok);
} return int 0;

// anim/box/box_paint draw a 40x30 box, so a surface smaller than that would
// turn their x0/y0 modulo arithmetic against a non-positive divisor; 80x60
// is the smallest round size that keeps it positive and leaves gfx_big_w a
// clean value to check.
gfx_big_setup() {
  sf_l_init(80, 60);
} return void;
():() then gfx_big_w:(80)
():() then idstd_sf_l_t_sum:(0)

gfx_big_w() {
  int w = (import sf_l_w);
} return int w;
